1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,720
Two brothers have the same parents, yet they

2
00:00:02,800 --> 00:00:04,240
can look quite different.

3
00:00:04,560 --> 00:00:07,520
Each began from one sperm and one egg,

4
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:09,600
and each of those carried just one set

5
00:00:09,640 --> 00:00:11,280
of chromosomes, not two.

6
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,160
So how does a cell halve its chromosome

7
00:00:14,240 --> 00:00:16,640
number, and make gametes that differ from each

8
00:00:16,720 --> 00:00:17,120
other?

9
00:00:18,334 --> 00:00:21,293
Suppose sperm and eggs were made by mitosis,

10
00:00:21,414 --> 00:00:22,333
like skin cells.

11
00:00:22,814 --> 00:00:26,253
Each would carry 46 chromosomes, so a fertilised

12
00:00:26,334 --> 00:00:28,813
egg would start with 92, and the next

13
00:00:29,134 --> 00:00:31,133
generation with 184.

14
00:00:31,774 --> 00:00:35,053
Gametes need one set, 23 chromosomes.

15
00:00:35,534 --> 00:00:37,293
A cell makes them with a different kind

16
00:00:37,374 --> 00:00:40,653
of division called meiosis: it copies its DNA

17
00:00:40,814 --> 00:00:42,973
once, then divides twice.

18
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:46,680
Here is our model cell again, two pairs

19
00:00:46,720 --> 00:00:47,640
of chromosomes.

20
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:50,280
This time we mark three genes.

21
00:00:50,760 --> 00:00:52,760
On the long pair, the upper gene has

22
00:00:52,840 --> 00:00:55,400
a plum version and a teal version, and

23
00:00:55,480 --> 00:00:56,600
so does the lower gene.

24
00:00:57,240 --> 00:00:59,800
The short pair has one gene marked, again

25
00:00:59,880 --> 00:01:02,040
with a plum version and a teal version.

26
00:01:02,680 --> 00:01:04,520
We label each pair of versions with a

27
00:01:04,600 --> 00:01:06,920
capital and a small letter, only to tell

28
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:07,720
them apart.

29
00:01:08,934 --> 00:01:13,653
Meiosis starts like mitosis: in interphase every chromosome

30
00:01:13,734 --> 00:01:16,373
is copied, so each one has two sister

31
00:01:16,494 --> 00:01:17,413
chromatids.

32
00:01:17,734 --> 00:01:19,693
Then comes the first difference.

33
00:01:19,974 --> 00:01:23,253
In prophase one, each chromosome finds its homologous

34
00:01:23,334 --> 00:01:26,133
partner, and the two lie side by side,

35
00:01:26,214 --> 00:01:27,493
gene against gene.

36
00:01:28,054 --> 00:01:30,293
That lets the cell later send one partner

37
00:01:30,374 --> 00:01:32,053
of each pair to each end.

38
00:01:33,267 --> 00:01:35,666
While they are paired, a chromatid from one

39
00:01:35,747 --> 00:01:38,786
chromosome and a chromatid from its partner break

40
00:01:38,867 --> 00:01:41,106
at the same place and swap the matching

41
00:01:41,187 --> 00:01:42,066
pieces.

42
00:01:42,387 --> 00:01:45,106
These two belong to different chromosomes, so they

43
00:01:45,187 --> 00:01:47,426
are called non-sister chromatids.

44
00:01:47,907 --> 00:01:49,586
This is crossing over.

45
00:01:50,800 --> 00:01:52,920
It does not make new alleles.

46
00:01:53,120 --> 00:01:55,760
It makes new combinations of the alleles already

47
00:01:55,840 --> 00:01:58,800
there, so the four long chromatids are now

48
00:01:58,880 --> 00:02:01,760
all different, even the sister chromatids.

49
00:02:02,967 --> 00:02:05,446
At the end of prophase one, the nuclear

50
00:02:05,527 --> 00:02:07,286
envelope breaks down.

51
00:02:07,447 --> 00:02:10,326
Then, in metaphase one, the pairs line up

52
00:02:10,407 --> 00:02:12,966
across the middle, with one partner facing each

53
00:02:13,047 --> 00:02:13,526
end.

54
00:02:14,734 --> 00:02:17,773
Which partner faces which end is chance, and

55
00:02:17,854 --> 00:02:19,453
each pair decides on its own.

56
00:02:20,014 --> 00:02:23,333
Here plum long and plum short face left.

57
00:02:23,614 --> 00:02:25,693
Another cell could line up with plum long

58
00:02:25,774 --> 00:02:27,213
and teal short on the left.

59
00:02:27,774 --> 00:02:29,933
This is independent assortment.

60
00:02:31,134 --> 00:02:34,093
In anaphase one, the fibres pull the homologous

61
00:02:34,294 --> 00:02:37,773
partners apart, but the centromeres do not split,

62
00:02:37,894 --> 00:02:40,973
so each chromosome still has two chromatids.

63
00:02:41,294 --> 00:02:43,693
Each end now holds one of each kind

64
00:02:43,734 --> 00:02:46,093
of chromosome, which is one set.

65
00:02:46,414 --> 00:02:50,013
Telophase and cytokinesis finish the first division, and

66
00:02:50,054 --> 00:02:52,453
there are two cells, each with half the

67
00:02:52,494 --> 00:02:53,453
chromosome number.

68
00:02:54,014 --> 00:02:56,173
That is how meiosis one halves it.

69
00:02:57,400 --> 00:03:00,280
Each cell goes straight into the second division.

70
00:03:00,520 --> 00:03:03,400
A gamete cannot keep doubled chromosomes, because it

71
00:03:03,480 --> 00:03:06,200
would carry twice the DNA of one set,

72
00:03:06,280 --> 00:03:08,840
so each cell divides again to share out

73
00:03:08,920 --> 00:03:11,280
the two chromatids of each chromosome.

74
00:03:11,560 --> 00:03:14,520
In metaphase two, each chromosome lines up on

75
00:03:14,600 --> 00:03:15,080
its own.

76
00:03:15,560 --> 00:03:18,600
In anaphase two, the centromeres split and the

77
00:03:18,680 --> 00:03:20,760
sister chromatids are pulled apart.

78
00:03:21,080 --> 00:03:23,480
Because of crossing over, the two chromatids of

79
00:03:23,507 --> 00:03:26,120
a chromosome can differ, so the two cells

80
00:03:26,200 --> 00:03:27,800
get different chromosomes.

81
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:32,440
So one cell has made four haploid cells,

82
00:03:32,520 --> 00:03:34,520
and in this cell the letters show that

83
00:03:34,600 --> 00:03:35,720
all four differ.

84
00:03:36,280 --> 00:03:38,280
Now switch crossing over off.

85
00:03:38,520 --> 00:03:41,000
Without it there would be only two kinds,

86
00:03:41,080 --> 00:03:42,120
each twice.

87
00:03:43,334 --> 00:03:45,493
Let's track the numbers in our model cell,

88
00:03:45,574 --> 00:03:47,493
which starts with four chromosomes.

89
00:03:47,974 --> 00:03:51,013
After copying, it still has four chromosomes, because

90
00:03:51,094 --> 00:03:54,733
we count centromeres, but now eight chromatids.

91
00:03:55,094 --> 00:03:57,253
After the first division, each of the two

92
00:03:57,414 --> 00:04:00,813
cells holds two chromosomes and four chromatids.

93
00:04:01,094 --> 00:04:03,893
After the second, each of four cells holds

94
00:04:03,974 --> 00:04:07,093
two chromosomes, and none has a sister chromatid,

95
00:04:07,214 --> 00:04:09,253
because the copies have been pulled apart.

96
00:04:09,574 --> 00:04:11,893
A human cell goes the same way, from

97
00:04:11,974 --> 00:04:15,253
46 chromosomes to 23 in each of four

98
00:04:15,334 --> 00:04:15,973
cells.

99
00:04:17,200 --> 00:04:20,240
How many combinations can assortment alone make?

100
00:04:20,480 --> 00:04:23,120
A pair's direction decides which partner goes into

101
00:04:23,160 --> 00:04:26,000
a particular gamete, and each pair decides on

102
00:04:26,120 --> 00:04:28,480
its own, so the choices multiply.

103
00:04:28,880 --> 00:04:31,200
Two pairs give two times two, which is

104
00:04:31,280 --> 00:04:31,680
4.

105
00:04:32,160 --> 00:04:34,160
With n pairs, it is 2 to the

106
00:04:34,240 --> 00:04:35,120
power n.

107
00:04:35,600 --> 00:04:38,480
A human has 23 pairs, so 2 to

108
00:04:38,520 --> 00:04:41,520
the power 23, more than 8 million.

109
00:04:42,080 --> 00:04:44,800
So two gametes from one person almost never

110
00:04:44,880 --> 00:04:47,200
match, but it is possible.

111
00:04:48,400 --> 00:04:53,200
Fertilisation joins two gametes, so 23 plus 23

112
00:04:53,440 --> 00:04:54,720
makes 46 again.

113
00:04:55,360 --> 00:04:59,120
Meiosis halves the number and fertilisation restores it,

114
00:04:59,200 --> 00:05:01,200
so the number stays the same in every

115
00:05:01,280 --> 00:05:02,160
generation.

116
00:05:03,367 --> 00:05:06,326
Mitosis is one division that makes two cells

117
00:05:06,407 --> 00:05:10,006
with the parent's chromosomes, identical, barring errors.

118
00:05:10,647 --> 00:05:14,006
Meiosis is two divisions after one copy, and

119
00:05:14,127 --> 00:05:16,486
makes four cells with half the number, and

120
00:05:16,567 --> 00:05:18,406
they are usually different from each other.

121
00:05:19,287 --> 00:05:22,486
The difference starts at the middle: in mitosis,

122
00:05:22,607 --> 00:05:24,646
each chromosome lines up on its own and

123
00:05:24,727 --> 00:05:26,486
its sister chromatids separate.

124
00:05:27,047 --> 00:05:30,166
In meiosis one, homologous pairs line up and

125
00:05:30,247 --> 00:05:31,526
the partners separate.

126
00:05:32,734 --> 00:05:36,173
Two questions, both on screen: the first about

127
00:05:36,214 --> 00:05:38,813
a pea plant, the second about a cell

128
00:05:38,894 --> 00:05:41,613
that is making gametes, part-way through a division.

129
00:05:42,094 --> 00:05:43,373
Pause here to answer.

130
00:05:53,800 --> 00:05:54,200
One.

131
00:05:54,440 --> 00:05:57,960
Each gamete holds 7 chromosomes, one from each

132
00:05:58,040 --> 00:05:58,440
pair.

133
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:01,400
Each pair can send either partner, so the

134
00:06:01,480 --> 00:06:04,600
choices multiply: 2 to the power 7, which

135
00:06:04,680 --> 00:06:06,400
is 128.

136
00:06:06,680 --> 00:06:09,960
The fertilised egg holds 14, because 7 plus

137
00:06:10,120 --> 00:06:10,360
7.

138
00:06:10,840 --> 00:06:13,720
If you said 14 combinations, you added up

139
00:06:13,800 --> 00:06:16,200
2 seven times instead of multiplying.

140
00:06:17,400 --> 00:06:17,800
Two.

141
00:06:18,040 --> 00:06:21,160
The chromosomes are lined up in homologous pairs,

142
00:06:21,240 --> 00:06:24,680
with no centromeres split, so this is metaphase

143
00:06:24,760 --> 00:06:26,120
of meiosis one.

144
00:06:26,440 --> 00:06:29,400
This division separates the partners, so each cell

145
00:06:29,480 --> 00:06:31,160
will hold 3 chromosomes.

146
00:06:31,720 --> 00:06:34,120
If you said 6, you kept the number,

147
00:06:34,160 --> 00:06:35,960
but meiosis one halves it.

148
00:06:37,167 --> 00:06:40,166
To keep it straight: meiosis one splits the

149
00:06:40,207 --> 00:06:43,566
homologous pairs, and that halves the number.

150
00:06:43,647 --> 00:06:46,766
Meiosis two splits the sister chromatids, the two

151
00:06:46,927 --> 00:06:49,006
halves of each doubled chromosome.

152
00:06:49,327 --> 00:06:52,766
Then fertilisation joins two gametes and restores the

153
00:06:52,847 --> 00:06:53,726
number.

154
00:06:54,287 --> 00:06:57,166
Next: how DNA is arranged in different kinds

155
00:06:57,247 --> 00:06:57,886
of cell.

156
00:06:59,100 --> 00:07:00,540
Here are the key notes.

157
00:07:00,780 --> 00:07:02,700
Pause the video if you need more time

158
00:07:02,780 --> 00:07:03,580
to copy them.
